{"title":"2018 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Coordinate_Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(AMC_10+_tactics)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (AMC 10+ tactics)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Not_for_use_in_a_time-limited_contest)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Not for use in a time-limited contest)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the cube <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/5/055dcba798b1424c44c2606d913e0f2edab1c5ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" /> with opposite vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/a/a0a1dbdebe8f3df1befc34f5862f69364b3c11b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"18\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/b/abb5588023cfa1ac14643e9778699f03eecc57a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$J$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are the midpoints of segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/6/a56a5efb54b46587c420e1101dc0ef393707a28d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{FB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/e/36e9272274bfd904964bafae7af78651bad5b540.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{HD},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"19\" /> respectively. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the ratio of the area of the cross-section <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/e/78ebd359b76a6a2b4da1a3027adc808e24a0277f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EJCI$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> to the area of one of the faces of the cube. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/9/41997da7bd47b28d5c0d22ebb5a00374e566592e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R^2?$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1d819cf8339fd4ae7e3d612b459da3b1e8cdc7b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(6cm); pair A,B,C,D,EE,F,G,H,I,J; C = (0,0); B = (-1,1); D = (2,0.5); A = B+D; G = (0,2); F = B+G; H = G+D; EE = G+B+D; I = (D+H)/2; J = (B+F)/2; filldraw(C--I--EE--J--cycle,lightgray,black); draw(C--D--H--EE--F--B--cycle);  draw(G--F--G--C--G--H); draw(A--B,dashed); draw(A--EE,dashed); draw(A--D,dashed); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(EE); dot(F); dot(G); dot(H); dot(I); dot(J); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,E); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,W); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,S); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,E); label(&quot;$E$&quot;,EE,N); label(&quot;$F$&quot;,F,W); label(&quot;$G$&quot;,G,N); label(&quot;$H$&quot;,H,E); label(&quot;$I$&quot;,I,E); label(&quot;$J$&quot;,J,W); [/asy]\" width=\"258\" height=\"285\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/a/36ad9b5b5b4e15be1bbefa7c0bfe63f953ec318a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\frac{5}{4} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } \\frac{4}{3} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } \\frac{3}{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } \\frac{25}{16} \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\frac{9}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"381\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/e/78ebd359b76a6a2b4da1a3027adc808e24a0277f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EJCI$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> is a rhombus by symmetry.\nLet the side length of the cube be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. By the Pythagorean theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/d/dbdfe927a8f77f142a5b38f0c71646dad12fd6b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EC= s\\sqrt 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/a/81ac306467d095e668d81ba97bafe44c0763e2ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$JI= s\\sqrt 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" />. Since the area of a rhombus is half the product of its diagonals, the area of the cross section is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/3/173aed16d9ca33c6005bde098e952717aded62f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{s^2\\sqrt 6}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"42\" height=\"41\" />. This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc8a87b39225efd82e69a6b285104636e2308bc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R = \\frac{\\sqrt 6}2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"65\" height=\"41\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/6/a46dc310e1700e891c77b1500b1c1a1060c2c448.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R^2 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{3}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"107\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This time, instead of using a variable like we did in Solution 1, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, for the side length as in the above solution, choose an easy value for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> such as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. In the above solution, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/2/752ccf51ef0be42644a0099346e876310df962f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" /> cancels out in the end, so ultimately the answers are equivalent. \n</p><p>~da bum Technobobbertjr\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Coordinate_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Coordinate_Geometry)\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the edges of the cube have same lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is the origin, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/4/fc4bd4aa9f2c27f19eb863b3d3bad8a04801997f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{AD }$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"19\" /> is the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> direction, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/8/1b86957aea55229fed62084ca16b9a6bfb1cfe10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{AE}$\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" /> is the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> direction, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/c/9ac92450d97a943ce4cdfb33be0ac46f625b10de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{AB }$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"19\" /> is the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> direction. Therefore, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/3/4137538883fe6755e43f5242d58830ef0287041c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(0,a,0), I(a,\\frac{a}{2},0), C(a,0,a),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"241\" height=\"34\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/c/cacf0504645fd1266f67e5a5ef1c806cebc53cae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$J(0,\\frac{a}{2},a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"76\" height=\"34\" />. Hence, we can figure out that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/2/e42a613ead8128be3de3fb8256d4560887dbc9c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|EC|=\\sqrt{a^2 + a^2 + a^2}=\\sqrt{3}a\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/5/54588403701c048c112889be3a47374f6d7d7773.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|IJ|=\\sqrt{a^2 + 0 + a^2}=\\sqrt{2}a\\]\" width=\"214\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/e/78ebd359b76a6a2b4da1a3027adc808e24a0277f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EJCI$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> is a rhombus, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/f/a2f3176bd8ddabb8a5dd2c23a8e7c0f23923146f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}a^2}{2a^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"138\" height=\"43\" />. Finally, we can see that the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/f/70f6ecaf9398596e0ebfcb6e65bea2107fbc1c74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R^2=\\frac{6}{4}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{3}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"144\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Bloggish\" title=\"User:Bloggish\">Bloggish</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28AMC_10.2B_tactics.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(AMC_10+_tactics)\">Solution 4 (AMC 10+ tactics)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (AMC 10+ tactics)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can solve this with 3D Cartesian coordinates. Assume WLOG that the sides of the square are of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the origin and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/9/a79b284a0644fe39e00dceb4a037b68c98ac9ec3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"19\" /> be the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> direction, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/0/b40274263a340bf03341a98e6f82322586672131.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CG}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> be the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> direction, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/2/842492e6cd0fbe06650a6296e9a8da8a78f7696f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"19\" /> be the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> direction. We find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/2/fe2da265d35fee769c336e11a456f81f2dada59f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I=(2,1,0),J=(0,1,2),E=(2,2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"295\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/1/bf18712be547d5eb0d16b3a7d7f99562f3ba77d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CI=CJ=EI=EJ=\\sqrt{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"221\" height=\"18\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/e/78ebd359b76a6a2b4da1a3027adc808e24a0277f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EJCI$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" /> is a rhombus. Furthermore, by the distance formula, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/3/1c30646bb304ee20308342ed75badfe557882ad5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IJ=\\sqrt8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>By the Law of Cosines on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/a/98a46bf8e866e9808f2055ea8f0a6604e50faac2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle JCI$\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/d/ecdd93e83cf4c500846050f27d926e257c64a713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos \\angle JCI=\\frac{\\sqrt5^2+ \\sqrt5^2-\\sqrt8^2}{2\\cdot \\sqrt5\\cdot \\sqrt5}=\\frac 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"279\" height=\"49\" />. By the Law of Cosines on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/d/4add36fd5219c330c502bb008b327b9a264fa58d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle JEI$\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/d/f7de298477faaf0e7b5a1e50f44a8a1c0476a847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos \\angle JEI=\\frac{\\sqrt5^2+ \\sqrt5^2-\\sqrt8^2}{2\\cdot \\sqrt5\\cdot \\sqrt5}=\\frac 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"279\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>Brahmagupta's formula states given a quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb60e0857a2c0a2c47acfbe81781032feb1f7150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c,d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/3/2f36523352161ef6d906a49bf80ea44b5b181dac.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[ABCD]=\\sqrt{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)-abcd\\cos^2\\left({\\frac{B+D}{2}}\\right)}\\]\" width=\"500\" height=\"53\" />where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/8/fe8aa84202349c5f233619bb3f1df1e140ddfcc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s=\\frac{a+b+c+d}2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"136\" height=\"37\" />. Using this formula, we find that<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/b/7fbe4b09c4a9c56ef72ab87055b0fa8675637a65.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[EJCI]=\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt5-\\sqrt5)(2\\sqrt5-\\sqrt5)(2\\sqrt5-\\sqrt5)(2\\sqrt5-\\sqrt5)-\\sqrt 5^4(\\tfrac 15)^2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\\]\" width=\"623\" height=\"32\" /> \n</p><p>Using Brahmagupta's formula again, we can find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7fcf5e83bf1706ead2d5684f9a22c34ff6ffae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABCD]=\\sqrt{(4-2)(4-2)(4-2)(4-2)-2^4\\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{90^\\circ+90^\\circ}2\\right)}=\\sqrt{16}=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"602\" height=\"53\" />. \n</p><p>The answer is thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/5/7351cb2ad8aaa87d03b7d5a399764db4af4f8628.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{4}\\right)^2=\\frac 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"108\" height=\"56\" /> so we circle answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/1/dc194f55d1def9ce1dad354fcea8b7797680fa65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{3}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"61\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~franzliszt\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Not_for_use_in_a_time-limited_contest.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Not_for_use_in_a_time-limited_contest)\">Solution 5 (Not for use in a time-limited contest)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Not for use in a time-limited contest)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will use the following\n</p><p>Theorem: Suppose we have a quadrilateral with edges of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb60e0857a2c0a2c47acfbe81781032feb1f7150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c,d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> (in that order) and diagonals of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/0/6f06a183d1092b27328c12a97322763ac8db97a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"26\" height=\"11\" />. Bretschneider's formula states that the area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/4/d74fe9b4608c09a5f19b35410d84cf675a66a940.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABCD]=\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{4p^2q^2-(b^2+d^2-a^2-c^2)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"353\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Proof: Here is one of my favorite proofs of this with vector geometry. \n</p><p>Suppose a quadrilateral has sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/2/d82fb3cd5abda50c77cb511084bed8673403b32a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a}, \\vec{b}, \\vec{c}, \\vec{d}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"63\" height=\"21\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/7/56792bbc0308bf387f6c8ad815a1562c17a0d0f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{a} + \\vec{b} + \\vec{c} + \\vec{d} = \\vec{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"136\" height=\"19\" /> and that the diagonals of the quadrilateral are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/4/114c8249230f212d372231c81dea87788c42f414.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{p} = \\vec{b} + \\vec{c} = -\\vec{a} - \\vec{d}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"156\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/5/ee53869afb684e8f20fbdb93cd45f5d6deb8d319.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{q} = \\vec{a} + \\vec{b} = -\\vec{c} - \\vec{d}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"154\" height=\"21\" />. The area of any such quadrilateral is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/6/c66ada088458f04a417a2584710af753561adec3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} |\\vec{p} \\times \\vec{q}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"61\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/8/8381820a9f5ac8713dab6aacab7282b165a28e9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K = \\frac{1}{2} |\\vec{p} \\times \\vec{q}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"102\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Lagrange's Identity states that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/d/1edefc699697c5dba07a4f8d8cdcd6c107c5b502.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\vec{a}|^2|\\vec{b}|^2-(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b})^2=|\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b}|^2 \\implies \\sqrt{|\\vec{a}|^2|\\vec{b}|^2-(\\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{b})^2}=|\\vec{a}\\times\\vec{b}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"474\" height=\"32\" />. Therefore:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/3/1636d33630f3466ebeaee1280893deef28fd1895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{|\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - (\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q})^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"200\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/f/79fe2a581182e39b035765c63949e60cb9f5eb04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - (2 \\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q})^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"196\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/c/9ccf2a4bc6104e21eeda53e7cbbb7b7d4fe7047b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [2 (\\vec{b} + \\vec{c}) \\cdot (\\vec{a} + \\vec{b})]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"280\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cd10bed20a058d3d09fb72d3daedceaa3722307.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [2 \\vec{b} \\cdot (\\vec{a} + \\vec{b}) + 2 \\vec{c} \\cdot (\\vec{a} + \\vec{b})]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"342\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/b/7eb3346ccc404f33b51b30ea40611de912046b5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [-2 \\vec{b} \\cdot (\\vec{c} + \\vec{d}) + 2 \\vec{c} \\cdot (\\vec{a} + \\vec{b})]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"356\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/f/1af05bc30058c961a26aa5d285509ea384c1e01c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [-2 \\vec{b} \\cdot \\vec{c} - 2 \\vec{b} \\cdot \\vec{d} + 2 \\vec{a} \\cdot \\vec{c} + 2 \\vec{b} \\cdot \\vec{c}]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"388\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/8/7588ef9fe40e58b9b4b50204f9f36cb37b06da53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [2 \\vec{a} \\cdot \\vec{c} - 2 \\vec{b} \\cdot \\vec{d}]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"253\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/9/809d0ee15c6a549f26a9787ad3fa3108dd0fc1d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - ([(\\vec{a} + \\vec{c})\\cdot(\\vec{a} + \\vec{c}) - \\vec{a}\\cdot\\vec{a} - \\vec{c}\\cdot\\vec{c}] - [(\\vec{b} + \\vec{d})\\cdot(\\vec{b} + \\vec{d}) - \\vec{b}\\cdot\\vec{b} - \\vec{d}\\cdot\\vec{d}])^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"650\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/9/8f9a0e6892f0904d04edeccf6fc479f026971989.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [|\\vec{b}|^2 + |\\vec{d}|^2 - |\\vec{a}|^2 - |\\vec{c}|^2 + (\\vec{a} + \\vec{c})\\cdot(\\vec{a} + \\vec{c}) - (\\vec{b} + \\vec{d})\\cdot(\\vec{b} + \\vec{d})]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"609\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/8/ef812fd4a088fe06d41088b1b9352db61e9b9714.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [|\\vec{b}|^2 + |\\vec{d}|^2 - |\\vec{a}|^2 - |\\vec{c}|^2 + |\\vec{a} + \\vec{c}|^2 - |\\vec{b} + \\vec{d}|^2]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"482\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/3/d937b4aa44995ee24f87d0ef66ecdbc567bcfd16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [|\\vec{b}|^2 + |\\vec{d}|^2 - |\\vec{a}|^2 - |\\vec{c}|^2 + |\\vec{a} + \\vec{c}|^2 - |-(\\vec{a} + \\vec{c})|^2]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"519\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a0661d14ddd63a4eff01539fbc07ac27044273.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4 |\\vec{p}|^2|\\vec{q}|^2 - [|\\vec{b}|^2 + |\\vec{d}|^2 - |\\vec{a}|^2 - |\\vec{c}|^2]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"325\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>Then, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/c/42c06de735d79f0b28c30bcbbcf96e67bb1ca42c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> represent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/a/caae136b98922d4260db50069eb107777b1fbb1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\vec{a}|, |\\vec{b}|, |\\vec{c}|, |\\vec{d}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"22\" /> (and are thus the side lengths) while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/0/6f06a183d1092b27328c12a97322763ac8db97a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"26\" height=\"11\" /> represent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/a/4eaf19210e4c17103aeecd53ce6b351d0645fe4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\vec{p}|, |\\vec{q}|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"18\" /> (and are thus the diagonal lengths), the area of a quadrilateral is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/d/60dc4dbcb38afe751b3438020e7edec259fdf9a0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[K = \\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{4p^2q^2 - (b^2 + d^2 - a^2 - c^2)^2}\\]\" width=\"288\" height=\"37\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Back to the problem. We use vectors. WLOG suppose the cube has sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the origin and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/9/a79b284a0644fe39e00dceb4a037b68c98ac9ec3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"19\" /> be the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> direction, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/0/b40274263a340bf03341a98e6f82322586672131.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CG}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> be the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> direction, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/2/842492e6cd0fbe06650a6296e9a8da8a78f7696f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"19\" /> be the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> direction. \n</p><p>Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/4/4d4558dd784c523d5726a970ecdc44c91a863918.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CI}=\\vec{JE}=\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"133\" height=\"64\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/8/b08d731fafa6049cd80ffa7b3aa41e697160b9cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CJ}=\\vec{IE}=\\begin{pmatrix} 0\\\\1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"133\" height=\"64\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/a/d2aa07886d6d88f9577ca537f53fc42bd458b6ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle JCI=\\theta$\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />. Dot product gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/b/d8ba6c113d4b94c832be8baba4d8e8f2e4381dd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos \\theta=\\left(\\frac{\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0\\\\1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}}{|\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}||\\begin{pmatrix} 0\\\\1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}|}\\right)=\\frac 15.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -62px\" width=\"249\" height=\"133\" />\n</p><p>Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/2/a322b02a28368c84e0241ab83346b756f6e17c3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec {IJ}=\\sqrt{|\\vec{CI}|+|\\vec{CJ}|-2\\cdot \\vec{CI}\\cdot \\vec{CJ}\\cos \\theta}=\\sqrt{|\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}|+|\\begin{pmatrix} 0\\\\1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}|-2\\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix} \\cdot\\begin{pmatrix} 0\\\\1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\frac 15} \\iff |\\vec{IJ}|=2\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -32px\" width=\"789\" height=\"77\" />.\n</p><p>Now, notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/a/ffad0f44a08fc1d3310f107b917d6bd53091cb9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{CE}=\\vec{CI}+\\vec{CJ}=\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}+\\begin{pmatrix} 0\\\\1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\2\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"311\" height=\"64\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1ba11b13a589b7d00dcf12019261095b8ffd7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\vec{CE}|=|\\begin{pmatrix} 2\\\\2\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}|=2\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"177\" height=\"64\" />.\n</p><p>Using Bretschneider's formula, we obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/8/cd8d489e996b3b2b64277b951dd8eba1d4854fd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[EJCI]=\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{4|\\vec{IJ}|^2|\\vec{CE}|^2-(|\\vec{CJ}|^2+|\\vec{IE}|^2-|\\vec{JE}|^2-|\\vec{CI}|^2)^2}=\\frac 14\\sqrt{4\\cdot (2\\sqrt2)^2\\cdot (2\\sqrt3)^2-(5^2+5^2-5^2-5^2)^2}=2\\sqrt6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"938\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Using Bretschneider's formula again, we can find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7fcf5e83bf1706ead2d5684f9a22c34ff6ffae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABCD]=\\sqrt{(4-2)(4-2)(4-2)(4-2)-2^4\\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{90^\\circ+90^\\circ}2\\right)}=\\sqrt{16}=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"602\" height=\"53\" />.\n</p><p>Hence, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/5/7351cb2ad8aaa87d03b7d5a399764db4af4f8628.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{4}\\right)^2=\\frac 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"108\" height=\"56\" /> so we circle answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>~franzliszt\n</p><p>Note: Of course this is a solution made by Franz Liszt, and also, holy shheeesshh this solution is long\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can simply plug in values and find \\( R \\) to eliminate the variables and avoid the complicated algebra. Let’s assume the side length of the cube is \\( 4 \\). The given figure is a rhombus because all its sides are equal, but the angles are not \\( 90^\\circ \\). To find the area of the rhombus, we need to calculate the lengths of the two diagonals.\n</p><p>The formula for the space diagonal of a cube is \\( \\sqrt{3}s \\), so \\( \\overline{CE} = 4\\sqrt{3} \\). By symmetry, \\( \\overline{IJ} = \\overline{BD} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\). Now, we can find the area of the rhombus using the formula for the area of a rhombus, which is the product of the diagonals divided by 2:\n</p><p>\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Area} &amp;= \\frac{4\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 4\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n&amp;= 8\\sqrt{6}.\n\\end{align*}\n</p><p>Next, the area of one of the cube’s faces is simply \\( 4^2 = 16 \\). So, \\( R \\) is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/8/018623a216265265fda896fb79cc2eb30ddba32d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\left(\\frac{8\\sqrt6}{16}\\right)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"75\" height=\"58\" />= <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/f/5afe33aae3fb93af4caf4e2ec175b338ce4623d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\frac{3}{2}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"55\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n-jb2015007\n</p><p>(Minor edits by FrankensteinQuixoteCabin)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2008 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 21\">Problem 21 of the 2008 AMC 10A</a> was nearly identical to this question, except that in this question you have to look for the square of the area, not the actual area.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=04pV_rZw8bg\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=04pV_rZw8bg</a>\n</p><p>~ Happytwin\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ji9_6XNxyIc\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ji9_6XNxyIc</a> \n</p><p>~ MathEx\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/FDgcLW4frg8?t=2823\">https://youtu.be/FDgcLW4frg8?t=2823</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/IrxtHlbERe8\">https://youtu.be/IrxtHlbERe8</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8\" title=\"2018 AMC 8\">2018 AMC 8</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems\" title=\"2018 AMC 8 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Answer_Key\" title=\"2018 AMC 8 Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=42&amp;year=2018\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2018 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 23\">Problem 23</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2018 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 25\">Problem 25</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2018 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2018 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2018_AMC_8_Problems/P